---
/**
 * EdgeLeaf - the first band: the sheet's headnote, printed by the frame.
 *
 * ─── WHAT IT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every page opens on the same printed record, and it says the four things a
 * reader deciding whether to stay actually needs, none of which the page says
 * about itself:
 *
 *   # SHEET 05␁OF 41   /DOC/RUST          PART:            03␁DOCUMENTATION
 *                                         CIRCULATION:               PUBLIC
 *                                         SOURCE:      pages/Doc/Rust.astro
 *                                         AS OF:                 2026-11-19
 *   ↳ THE RUST CORE, ITS BOUNDARIES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN
 *
 * It is the collation mark of a dossier: the sheet's number in the document, its
 * route, the part it belongs to, the day its facts were read, whether the build
 * asks search engines to index it, and the file it was set from. A printed
 * chapter opening carries the same five things above the drop - and a reader who
 * has seen one page of this site recognises the edge of every other.
 *
 * ─── THE FACTS ARE ROWS, AND THE ROWS HAVE ONE ORDER ──────────────────────
 * The facts were one inline run of `KEY: value` pairs. A run has no break
 * control, so at the paper's edge the line maker broke the last word it could
 * reach - `05␁CONTACT` printed as `CONTAC` + `T` - and the four facts read as a
 * sentence that happened to contain colons. They are a definition list, and the
 * stylesheet prints them as one: a key column, a right-aligned value column and
 * a hairline between rows (`Component/Layout/_Edge.scss`).
 *
 * The order is DATA, declared here in `FactOrder`, and not in any page: a page
 * supplies facts and the component decides their grammar, so all forty-one
 * leaves of the site agree. It reads as a sentence about the sheet -
 *
 *   PART:          what this is          (the reader's first question)
 *   CIRCULATION:   who may read it       (the second)
 *   SOURCE:        where it came from    (provenance follows identity)
 *   AS OF:         when it was read      (the stamp, LAST: it dates the set)
 *   AGE:           how old that makes it (the stale arm's own line, under it)
 *
 * - which is the colophon rule: a date belongs at the end of what it dates, not
 * in the middle. The date used to sit second, between the part and the two
 * facts it dates.
 *
 * ─── WHY THE FRAME PRINTS IT AND NOT THE PAGE ─────────────────────────────
 * A page knows its own content and not its place. Only the frame knows what is
 * before it and after it, so only the frame can number it - and a page that
 * numbered itself would be a page that could disagree with the index. `Base.astro`
 * mounts this once, above `<slot />`, so every route in the build opens the same
 * way, including the ones that never wear a shell.
 *
 * ─── WHAT IT IS NOT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * - It is not a band. No heading, no fill, no measure of its own: it is the
 *   sheet's head, at the frame's width, above whatever the page's first movement
 *   is. The homepage's opening pair and the Download masthead stay the first
 *   artifacts of their pages, byte for byte; this sits above them the way a
 *   running head sits above a drop head.
 * - It is not a second running head. The header's strip says which PART the
 *   reader is in and what the document's status is; this says which SHEET, when
 *   it was read, and what it was set from. A page that printed the part twice in
 *   28px would be printing one fact twice.
 * - It is not the shell's declaration strip. `PageShell` declares the GRID a page
 *   is composed on (`# LAYOUT / READING`); this declares the SHEET. A page may
 *   carry both, because they answer different questions, and the two are set
 *   differently on purpose: the declaration is a two-row block in the page's own
 *   column, the leaf is one line at the frame's edge with its gloss hung under it.
 *
 * ─── THE ADVERSE ARMS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Nine conditions are the design's second half (`Design-Brief.md` §7). The leaf
 * carries the ones an edge can actually meet, composed in the same strip rather
 * than in a box beside it:
 *
 *   unfiled    the page is not a sheet of the document     `◎ NOT FILED`, `PART: ◎ NONE`
 *   stale      the sheet's facts are older than the build  `◎ STALE` + `AGE: n DAYS`
 *   partial    a fact this build cannot state              `UNAVAILABLE`, never `0`
 *   denied     the build asks not to be indexed            `CIRCULATION: ◎ NOT INDEXED`
 *   loading    no spinner: the keys stay, the values bar    `WAITING ON: BUILD`
 *   too long   a route that will not fit                   the route wraps in full
 *   too much   a document past two digits                  the ordinal widens, and says so
 *
 * `empty`, `error` and `missing` are the turn's, because they are conditions of
 * what comes NEXT, not of what this sheet is.
 *
 * No script and no hydration: a headnote is printed.
 */
import {
	EdgePage,
	EdgeBuildDate,
	EdgeDaysBetween,
	EdgeStaleAfterDays,
} from "../../../Content/Edge/Copy.js";
import {
	EdgeExtent,
	EdgePartOf,
	EdgePlaceOf,
	EdgePropositionOf,
} from "../../../Content/Edge/Register.js";
import type { FrameSection } from "../Frame/Data.js";
import { GetI18n } from "../../../Library/I18n/Server";

interface Props {
	/** The path the reader asked for, printed when the page is unfiled. */
	path: string;

	/** The canonical path the build publishes - what the register is keyed on. */
	canonical: string;

	/** Whether the frame asks search engines not to index this page. */
	noIndex?: boolean;

	/**
	 * The page's own description, used as the `↳` line only when the register has
	 * no proposition for the sheet - a collection article, whose sentence is its
	 * own excerpt. The register wins wherever it speaks.
	 */
	description?: string;

	/** The part of the document, resolved by the frame. */
	section: FrameSection;

	/**
	 * Force one of the leaf's composed arms. The frame never sets it: it exists so
	 * a page that prints the edges as specimens (`/Specimen`) can mount the arm it
	 * is describing. Omitted, the arm is derived from the facts.
	 */
	state?:
		| "rest"
		| "unfiled"
		| "stale"
		| "loading"
		| "partial"
		| "too-long"
		| "too-much";

	/** Extra classes from the page. */
	class?: string;
}

const {
	path,
	canonical,
	noIndex = false,
	description,
	section,
	state: ForcedState,
	class: Class,
} = Astro.props;

const T = GetI18n();

const Copy = EdgePage(T);

const Place = EdgePlaceOf(canonical);

/**
 * The part, from the register when the sheet is filed and from the frame when it
 * is not.
 *
 * The register is the authority because it is the file that says where a sheet
 * sits; the frame's answer is used only for an unfiled page, where it is `◎
 * UNFILED` and the leaf prints `PART: ◎ NONE` anyway. (`/Specimen` is the case
 * that made this matter: the frame files it under no part, the register files it
 * with the index, and two answers to one question is the drift this site keeps
 * paying for.)
 */
const Part = Place === undefined ? section : EdgePartOf(Place.Sheet);

/** The sheet's own date, or the build's - never an invented one. */
const AsOf = Place?.Sheet.AsOf ?? EdgeBuildDate;

/** How old the sheet's date is, in whole days. */
const Age = EdgeDaysBetween(AsOf, EdgeBuildDate);

/** A sheet older than a quarter says so before the reader reads a word. */
const IsStale =
	Place !== undefined &&
	Place.Sheet.AsOf !== undefined &&
	Age > EdgeStaleAfterDays;

/**
 * The route as the grammar prints it: `/DOC/RUST`, uppercase, leading slash.
 *
 * The cover sheet's route is `/`, and the edges print it as `/LAND`: the grammar's
 * route form needs a name after the slash, and `LAND` is the document's own name
 * for itself (`# LAND / RECORD END` in the footer). A lone slash at 18px reads as
 * a separator, which is the one thing a route may not be mistaken for.
 */
const RouteText = (
	Place?.Sheet.Path === "/" ? "/LAND" : (Place?.Sheet.Path ?? path)
).toUpperCase();

/**
 * The proposition: the register's, then the page's own description, then honest
 * absence.
 *
 * A collection sheet (`/Doc/:Slug`, `/Blog/:Slug`) inverts the order: its row in
 * the register names the FAMILY ("one chapter of the manual"), and the page's own
 * description is the chapter's sentence. The register's line is the fallback
 * there, because a leaf that gave 78 articles one sentence would be a caption.
 */
const RegisterProposition = Place ? EdgePropositionOf(Place.Sheet) : "";

const OwnDescription = description?.trim() ?? "";

const Proposition =
	Place === undefined
		? Copy.leaf.unfiledProposition
		: Place.Sheet.Family !== undefined && OwnDescription !== ""
			? OwnDescription
			: RegisterProposition !== ""
				? RegisterProposition
				: OwnDescription !== ""
					? OwnDescription
					: Copy.leaf.unavailable;

/** The arm this leaf is printed in. A forced state wins; otherwise the facts decide. */
const State =
	ForcedState ??
	(Place === undefined
		? "unfiled"
		: IsStale
			? "stale"
			: RouteText.length > 48
				? "too-long"
				: "rest");

/** Whether the ordinal has outgrown its two digits. */
const IsTooMuch = Place !== undefined && EdgeExtent.length > 2;

/** The ordinal slot, printed with the grammar's fallback when there is no place. */
const Ordinal =
	State === "unfiled"
		? Copy.leaf.unfiled
		: (Place?.Ordinal ?? Copy.leaf.unfiled);

/** The extent slot: `OF 41`, or nothing when the page has no place in the series. */
const Extent = State === "unfiled" ? "" : `${Copy.leaf.of}\u2001${EdgeExtent}`;

/** The part's value: `03␁DOCUMENTATION`, or the fallback mark. */
const PartValue =
	State === "unfiled" ? Copy.leaf.noPart : `${Part.Index}\u2001${Part.Label}`;

/** The circulation fact: a published page, or one the build asks not to index. */
const Circulation = noIndex
	? Copy.leaf.circulationDenied
	: Copy.leaf.circulationPublic;

/** The file the sheet was set from, or where an unfiled page came from. */
const Source =
	State === "unfiled"
		? Copy.leaf.requestUrl
		: (Place?.Sheet.Source ?? Copy.leaf.unavailable);

/**
 * ─── THE CANONICAL ORDER, AND IT IS DATA ──────────────────────────────────
 * The four facts are a sentence about the sheet, and the sentence has one
 * order: what this is, who may read it, where it came from, and - last - when
 * it was read. The date goes last because it dates the set above it, not the
 * part beside it; it used to sit second, between identity and provenance.
 *
 * It is declared HERE and not in a page. A page supplies facts; the component
 * decides their grammar, so no page can reintroduce a different order and the
 * forty-one leaves of the site cannot disagree. `age` is the stale arm's own
 * line and follows the date it ages - it is the only fact that is not always
 * printed, and the sequence has to have a place for it whether it prints or
 * not.
 */
const FactOrder = ["part", "circulation", "source", "as-of", "age"] as const;

/** One fact's kind: its slot in the register, and the key of its rule set. */
type FactKind = (typeof FactOrder)[number];

/**
 * A fact is its own row so the stylesheet can set a figure tabular, a part's
 * name unbreakable and a path breakable at its own slashes - without touching
 * the words.
 */
type Fact = {
	readonly key: string;
	readonly value: string;
	readonly kind: FactKind;
};

const Facts: readonly Fact[] = (
	[
		{ key: Copy.leaf.part, value: PartValue, kind: "part" },
		{
			key: Copy.leaf.asOf,
			value:
				IsStale && State !== "unfiled"
					? `${AsOf}\u2001${Copy.leaf.stale}`
					: AsOf,
			kind: "as-of",
		},
		...(IsStale && State !== "unfiled"
			? [
					{
						key: Copy.leaf.age,
						value: `${Age}\u2001DAYS`,
						kind: "age" as const,
					},
				]
			: []),
		{ key: Copy.leaf.circulation, value: Circulation, kind: "circulation" },
		{ key: Copy.leaf.source, value: Source, kind: "source" },
	] satisfies Fact[]
).sort((A, B) => FactOrder.indexOf(A.kind) - FactOrder.indexOf(B.kind));
---

<div
	class:list={["EdgeLeaf", Class]}
	data-state={State}
	data-part={Part.Key}
	data-circulation={noIndex ? "denied" : "public"}
>
	<div class="EdgeLeaf__row">
		<div class="EdgeLeaf__head">
			<p class="EdgeLeaf__folio">
				<span class="EdgeLeaf__mark" aria-hidden="true">
					#
				</span>
				<span class="EdgeLeaf__sheet">{Copy.leaf.sheet}</span>
				<span class="EdgeLeaf__ordinal">{Ordinal}</span>
				{Extent !== "" ? (
					<span class="EdgeLeaf__of">{Extent}</span>
				) : null}
			</p>

			{/*
			 * The route breaks at its own slashes and nowhere else. A `<wbr>` is
			 * zero-width, is not part of the copied text, and is the only place
			 * the line maker may take a break inside the route - so a long
			 * route wraps at a separator instead of splitting a segment
			 * (`/CONTAC` + `T`).
			 */}
			<p class="EdgeLeaf__route" data-route={RouteText}>
				{RouteText.split("/").map((Segment, Position, Segments) =>
					Position < Segments.length - 1
						? [Segment, "/", <wbr />]
						: [Segment],
				)}
			</p>
		</div>

		<dl class="EdgeLeaf__facts">
			{Facts.map((Fact) => (
				<div class="EdgeLeaf__fact" data-kind={Fact.kind}>
					<dt class="EdgeLeaf__key">{Fact.key}</dt>
					<dd
						class="EdgeLeaf__value"
						data-figure={Fact.kind === "age" ? "" : undefined}
					>
						{State === "loading" ? (
							<span class="EdgeLeaf__bar" aria-hidden="true" />
						) : Fact.kind === "source" ? (
							/*
							 * A source is a path, and a path's slashes are its own
							 * break points - the `Detail/Folio` rule for the same
							 * reason: a path is data, and data breaks where its
							 * author put the marks.
							 */
							Fact.value
								.split("/")
								.map((Segment, Position, Segments) =>
									Position < Segments.length - 1
										? [Segment, "/", <wbr />]
										: [Segment],
								)
						) : (
							Fact.value
						)}
					</dd>
				</div>
			))}
		</dl>
	</div>

	<p class="EdgeLeaf__proposition">
		<span class="EdgeLeaf__proposition-mark" aria-hidden="true"><span class="MarkRelation"></span></span>
		<span class="EdgeLeaf__proposition-text">{Proposition}</span>
	</p>

	{State === "loading" ? (
		<p class="EdgeLeaf__note">{`WAITING ON: ${Copy.leaf.waitingOn}`}</p>
	) : null}

	{State === "stale" ? (
		<p class="EdgeLeaf__note">{Copy.leaf.staleNote}</p>
	) : null}

	{State === "too-long" ? (
		<p class="EdgeLeaf__note">{Copy.leaf.tooLongNote}</p>
	) : null}

	{State === "too-much" || IsTooMuch ? (
		<p class="EdgeLeaf__note">{Copy.leaf.tooMuchNote}</p>
	) : null}

	{State === "partial" ? (
		<p class="EdgeLeaf__note">{`${Copy.leaf.source}\u2001${Copy.leaf.unavailable}`}</p>
	) : null}
</div>
